## 1. INTRODUCTION: THE SCHRÖDINGER TRANSPOSITION

In 1917, Albert Einstein introduced the cosmological constant $\lambda$ into his field equations to maintain a static universe—a belief that was standard at the time. The constant appeared on the left-hand side of the equation, as a modification to the geometry of space-time:

$$R_{\mu\nu} - \frac{1}{2} g_{\mu\nu} R - \lambda g_{\mu\nu} = -\kappa T_{\mu\nu}$$

Shortly thereafter, in a short paper published in 1918, Erwin Schrödinger performed a simple algebraic transposition. He moved the cosmological constant term to the right-hand side of the equation:

$$R_{\mu\nu} - \frac{1}{2} g_{\mu\nu} R = -\kappa \left( T_{\mu\nu} + \frac{\lambda}{\kappa} g_{\mu\nu} \right)$$

This was not a mathematical innovation—it was a reinterpretation. The constant that had been a geometric term, a property of space-time itself, was now a source term, a property of the energy content of the universe. Schrödinger had transformed the cosmological constant from a "fudge factor" into a physical force: **anti-gravity**.

This transposition reveals the fundamental duality that modern cosmology has obscured: gravity and anti-gravity are two sides of the same coin. The cosmological constant is not a property of geometry—it is a property of the vacuum. It is the manifestation of anti-gravity.

